"On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41278view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":520}); Total lines: 1284\nL442:  | $$\\Phi_{G}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X},\\qquad\\Phi_{G}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\bigr).$$  |\nL443: \nL444: For any fixed realization $G=g$, $\\Phi_{g}$ is deterministic.\nL445: #### Draft trees.\nL446: \nL447: At speculative round $t$, a drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ returns a finite rooted tree\nL448: \nL449:  | $$\\mathcal{T}_{t}=(\\mathcal{N}_{t},\\mathcal{E}_{t})$$  |\nL450: \nL451: whose root is the current committed state $x_{t}$. Every edge $(y,z)\\in\\mathcal{E}_{t}$ corresponds to exactly one hypothetical commitment:\nL452: \nL453:  | $$z=y\\oplus(s,v)$$  |\nL454: \nL455: for some $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(y)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$.\nL456: The exactness arguments make no assumption about how this tree is constructed. In particular, $\\mathcal{D}$ may be learned or hand-designed, may return a chain or a tree, may depend on all previous target outputs, and at $T>0$ may depend on the shared randomness $G$.\nL457: #### State-equality verifier.\nL458: \nL459: Let $\\Phi$ denote $\\Phi_{0}$ for $T=0$ or $\\Phi_{G}$ after fixing the shared randomness for $T>0$. Beginning at the root $y_{0}=x_{t}$, the verifier proceeds recursively.\nL460: \nL461: If $y_{j}$ has a drafted child $z$ such that\nL462: \nL463:  | $$z=\\Phi(y_{j}),$$  |\nL464: the verifier accepts that child and continues from it. If no such child exists, it rejects all remaining descendants, commits $\\Phi(y_{j})$ directly, and terminates the speculative round. If $y_{j}$ is terminal, no further transition is made.\nL465: \nL466: Since $\\Phi(y_{j})$ is a unique one-step extension, at most one child of a node can be accepted. Hence the accepted portion of an arbitrary draft tree is a single path.\nL467: Let $X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$ denote the complete draft-free trajectory and $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}$ the complete committed speculative trajectory. A verifier is distribution preserving when\nL468: \nL469:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$$  |\nL470: \nL471: for every admissible drafter $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL472: ### A.2 Proof of Proposition cite153†1 L473: ###### Proof.\nL474: \nL475: Fix target $p_{\\theta}$, causal context $C$, initial block state $x_{0}$, and an arbitrary drafter $\\mathcal{D}$. By the fixed deterministic tie-breaking rule, at $T=0$ the target transition\nL476: \nL477:  | $$\\Phi_{0}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X}$$  |\nL478: \nL479: is deterministic and gives a unique successor for every nonterminal state.\nL480: \nL481: Let\nL482: \nL483:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}=x_{0},\\qquad x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k+1}=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k})$$  |\nL484: be the draft-free trajectory, and let $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$ denote the state of the speculative decoder after $k$ committed transitions.\nL485: \nL486: We prove by induction on $k$ that\nL487: \nL488:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k}$$  |\nL489: \nL490: for every commitment before termination.\nL491: #### Base case.\nL492: \nL493: For $k=0$, both procedures start from the same initial state:\nL494: \nL495:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{0}=x_{0}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}.$$  |\nL496: #### Inductive step.\nL497: \nL498: Assume\nL499: \nL500:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k}$$  |\nL501: \nL502: for some nonterminal state. From $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$, the target has the unique successor\nL503: \nL504:  | $$\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}).$$  |\nL505: \nL506: If the draft tree contains this state as a child of $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$, state-equality verification accepts that child. Otherwise, the verifier rejects the speculative continuation and commits the same state as the target fallback. Hence, in either case,\nL507:  | $\\displaystyle x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k})$  |\nL508:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k})$  |\nL509:  |  | $\\displaystyle=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k+1}.$  |\nL510: \nL511: Thus the induction hypothesis holds for $k+1$.\nL512: \nL513: Since $\\Phi_{0}(x)$ is unique, at most one child of any tree node can be accepted. Therefore, even for an arbitrary finite draft tree, the accepted states form a single path, and the argument above applies to every committed transition.\nL514: \nL515: By induction,\nL516:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{0,\\mathcal{D}}=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}.$$  |\nL517: \nL518: ∎\nL519: \nL520: The argument is block-local only for notation. Since the completed block is identical to draft-free decoding, the causal context of the next block is also identical. Induction over blocks therefore gives equality of the complete generated sequence at $T=0$.\nL521: ### A.3 Proof of Proposition cite154†2 L522: ###### Proof.\nL523: \nL524: Fix $T>0$, target $p_{\\theta}$, causal context $C$, initial state $x_{0}$, and an arbitrary drafter $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL525: \nL526: The proof has three steps. First, we show that the shared-Gumbel construction induces the same draft-free trajectory distribution as ordinary temperature-$T$ sampling. Second, conditioned on a realization of the Gumbel table, verification reduces to the deterministic case of Proposition cite153†1 . Third, we marginalize over the shared randomness.\nL527: #### Step 1: Gumbel-driven draft-free law.\nL528: \nL529: Fix a nonterminal state $x$ and let\nL530: \nL531:  | $$s=s^{\\star}(x).$$  |\nL532: \nL533: Since $\\{G_{a(s),v}\\}_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}$ are independent standard Gumbels, the Gumbel–argmax identity gives\nL534:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pr[v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=u]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp\\!\\left(T^{-1}\\log p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)\\right)}{\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\exp\\!\\left(T^{-1}\\log p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)\\right)}$  |  | (5)\nL535:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}{\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}$  |  | (6)\nL536:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\pi_{T,s}(u\\mid x,C).$  |  | (7)\nL537: \nL538: Thus, for any fixed state, the Gumbel construction induces exactly the temperature-$T$ target token law.\nL539: It remains to show that using a single Gumbel table indexed by absolute position preserves this law along the full adaptive trajectory. Consider commitment step $j$ of Gumbel-driven draft-free decoding, with current state $x_{j}$ and selected position\nL540: \nL541:  | $$s_{j}=s^{\\star}(x_{j}).$$  |\nL542: Let $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$ denote the history generated by the states and Gumbel rows used during the first $j$ commitments. Since each commitment fills a previously masked position, $s_{j}$ has not been committed before, and therefore the row\nL543: \nL544:  | $$G_{a(s_{j}),\\cdot}$$  |\nL545: \nL546: has not previously been used.\nL547: Moreover, $s_{j}$ is determined by $x_{j}$ and hence by $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$; its selection does not depend on any unused Gumbel row. Since the Gumbel rows are independent across absolute positions, $G_{a(s_{j}),\\cdot}$ remains an independent standard-Gumbel vector conditional on $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$. Therefore,\nL548: \nL549:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[v^{\\star}_{G}(x_{j})=u\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{j}\\right]=\\pi_{T,s_{j}}(u\\mid x_{j},C).$$  |\nL550: Hence the Gumbel-driven decoder has the same conditional transition law as ordinary draft-free sampling at every commitment. Since both start from $x_{0}$, the chain rule gives\nL551: \nL552:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}.$$  |  | (8)\nL553: #### Step 2: conditional pathwise exactness.\nL554: \nL555: Fix any realization $G=g$. Then\nL556: \nL557:  | $$\\Phi_{g}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X}$$  |\nL558: \nL559: is a deterministic transition map.\nL560: \nL561: The proof of Proposition cite153†1 requires only that the target have a unique deterministic successor at each state and that a drafted child be accepted exactly when it equals that successor. Both properties hold with $\\Phi_{g}$ in place of $\\Phi_{0}$. Therefore, for every realization $g$,\nL562:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g).$$  |  | (9)\nL563: \nL564: This argument is independent of how $\\mathcal{D}$ constructs its draft. In particular, the draft may depend on stale target distributions, learned parameters, previous decoding history, or the shared table $G$ itself; a drafted edge is committed only when it equals the target successor under the same realization $g$.\nL565: #### Step 3: marginalization.\nL566: \nL567: By Step 2, for every realization $g$ of the shared Gumbel table,\nL568: \nL569:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g).$$  |\nL570: \nL571: Therefore, when $G$ is drawn according to its Gumbel distribution,\nL572: \nL573:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(G)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G).$$  |\nL574: \nL575: By definition, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}$ denotes this same random speculative trajectory with its dependence on $G$ left implicit.\nL576: \nL577: Hence, for any set $A$ of complete decoding trajectories,\nL578:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\in A\\right]=\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\in A\\right].$$  |\nL579: \nL580: Using equation cite243†8 ,\nL581: \nL582:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\in A\\right]=\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}\\in A\\right].$$  |\nL583: \nL584: Since this holds for every $A$,\nL585: \nL586:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}.$$  |\nL587: Finally, for fixed $G$, the committed trajectory is the deterministic orbit of $\\Phi_{G}$ and is therefore invariant to the choice of drafter. This establishes drafter-invariance.\nL588: \nL589: ∎\nL590: ## Appendix B Rejection Sampling as an Alternative Verifier\nL591: Coupled-noise match is the verifier used by SPOT because it requires only state equality and applies directly to DDTree. If a drafter instead exposes an explicit sequential proposal distribution, exact verification can alternatively be performed by rejection sampling (RS). We formalize this alternative here, prove its correctness and maximal per-link acceptance probability, and describe the implementation-level validation used in our experiments.\nL592: ### B.1 Sequential proposal setup\nL593: \nL594: Consider a verified predecessor state\nL595: \nL596:  | $$y_{k}\\in\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}.$$  |\nL597: \nL598: Let\nL599: \nL600:  | $$\\mathcal{Z}(y_{k})=\\mathcal{M}(y_{k})\\times\\mathcal{V}$$  |\nL601: \nL602: be the set of possible one-step position–token commitments.\nL603: #### Target commit law.\nL604: \nL605: Let\nL606: \nL607:  | $$s_{k}^{\\star}=s^{\\star}(y_{k}).$$  |\nL608: \nL609: At $T>0$, the target distribution over one-step commitments is\nL610: \nL611:  | $$\\pi_{k}(s,v)=\\mathbf{1}\\{s=s_{k}^{\\star}\\}\\,\\pi_{T,s_{k}^{\\star}}(v\\mid y_{k},C).$$  |  | (10)\nL612: \nL613: Therefore\nL614: \nL615:  | $$\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal{Z}(y_{k})}\\pi_{k}(z)=1.$$  |\nL616: #### Proposal law.\nL617: \nL618: Suppose the drafter exposes a normalized proposal distribution\nL619: \nL620:  | $$q_{k}\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{Z}(y_{k}))$$  |\nL621: \nL622: and generates one proposed commitment\nL623: \nL624:  | $$\\widetilde{Z}_{k}=(\\widetilde{S}_{k},\\widetilde{V}_{k})\\sim q_{k}.$$  |  | (11)\nL625: \nL626: For the chain proposal used in our experiments,\nL627: \nL628:  | $$q_{k}(s,v)=q_{k}^{\\mathrm{pos}}(s)\\,\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,k,s}(v),$$  |  | (12)\nL629: where $q_{k}^{\\mathrm{pos}}$ is the drafter’s next-position distribution and $\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,k,s}$ is its stale temperature-adjusted token conditional.\nL630: \nL631: The factorization in equation cite244†12 is not required by the proof; exactness needs only that the proposal actually be sampled from the explicit distribution $q_{k}$ supplied to the verifier.\nL632: #### Accept–residual rule.\nL633: \nL634: Given proposal $\\widetilde{Z}_{k}=z$ with $q_{k}(z)>0$, accept it with probability\nL635: \nL636:  | $$\\alpha_{k}(z)=\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{\\pi_{k}(z)}{q_{k}(z)}\\right\\}.$$  |  | (13)\nL637: \nL638: Upon rejection, draw a replacement from\nL639: \nL640:  | $$r_{k}(z)=\\frac{(\\pi_{k}(z)-q_{k}(z))_{+}}{\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal{Z}(y_{k})}(\\pi_{k}(w)-q_{k}(w))_{+}}.$$  |  | (14)\nL641: \nL642: If the denominator is zero, then $\\pi_{k}=q_{k}$, rejection has probability zero, and $r_{k}$ is never queried.\nL643: \nL644: ### B.2 Exactness and optimal acceptance\nL645: ###### Proposition 3 (Rejection-sampling exactness and optimal acceptance).\nL646: \nL647: Fix a verified predecessor state $y_{k}$. Let $\\widetilde{Z}_{k}\\sim q_{k}$ be accepted according to equation cite245†13 , with rejected proposals replaced using equation cite246†14 . Then the committed pair $Z_{k}$ satisfies\nL648: \nL649:  | $$Z_{k}\\sim\\pi_{k}.$$  |\nL650: \nL651: Moreover,\nL652: \nL653:  | $$\\Pr[\\mathrm{accept}\\mid y_{k}]=1-D_{\\mathrm{TV}}(\\pi_{k},q_{k}),$$  |\nL654: which is the maximal possible agreement probability among all couplings with marginals $\\pi_{k}$ and $q_{k}$. Applying the rule sequentially at every verified predecessor therefore reproduces the complete draft-free target distribution.\nL655: ###### Proof.\nL656: \nL657: Fix $y_{k}$ and abbreviate\nL658: \nL659:  | $$\\pi=\\pi_{k},\\qquad q=q_{k},\\qquad r=r_{k}.$$  |\nL660: #### Step 1: accepted mass.\nL661: \nL662: For arbitrary $z\\in\\mathcal{Z}(y_{k})$ with $q(z)>0$,\nL663: \nL664:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pr[\\widetilde{Z}_{k}=z,\\mathrm{accept}]$  | $\\displaystyle=q(z)\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{\\pi(z)}{q(z)}\\right\\}$  |  | (15)\nL665:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\min\\{q(z),\\pi(z)\\}.$  |  | (16)\nL666: \nL667: If $q(z)=0$, both sides of equation cite247†16 are zero, so the identity holds for all $z$.\nL668: \nL669: Summing over $z$,\nL670: \nL671:  | $$\\Pr[\\mathrm{accept}]=\\sum_{z}\\min\\{q(z),\\pi(z)\\}.$$  |  | (17)\nL672: \nL673: Hence\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDon’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41278view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":390}); Total lines: 600\nL352:   * Zhou et al. (2024) S. Zhou, F. F. Xu, H. Zhu, X. Zhou, R. Lo, A. Sridhar, X. Cheng, Y. Bisk, D. Fried, U. Alon, et al. WebArena: a realistic web environment for building autonomous agents. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), Cited by: cite134†§2 , cite156†§3.1 , cite155†§4.1 .\nL353: ## Appendix A Method Details\nL354: ### A.1 Adapter Factorization\nL355: \nL356: The factors of an adapter are not unique. For any invertible $Q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}$,\nL357: \nL358:  | $$(QA_{\\ell},\\;B_{\\ell}Q^{-1})\\quad\\text{induces}\\quad(B_{\\ell}Q^{-1})(QA_{\\ell})=B_{\\ell}A_{\\ell},$$  |  | (9)\nL359: so different factor pairs give the same update and the same policy. Two adapters trained on similar data can therefore lie far apart in factor space and still behave alike. WebMix combines adapters through their updates $\\Delta W_{\\ell}$, which Eq. cite116†9 leaves unchanged. Every comparison we report is either functional (task success) or computed on the updates $\\Delta W_{\\ell}$ (Figures cite127†4(a) and cite128†5 ), never on the factors.\nL360: ### A.2 KL Anchor\nL361: \nL362:  | $\\displaystyle\\tilde{d}^{(k)}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle:=\\operatorname{clip}\\!\\left(\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{i}\\mid x,y_{<i})-\\log\\pi^{(k)}(y_{i}\\mid x,y_{<i}),\\ \\tau_{\\min},\\ \\tau_{\\max}\\right),$  |  | (10)\nL363:  | $\\displaystyle\\kappa^{(k)}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle:=\\exp\\big(\\tilde{d}^{(k)}_{i}\\big)-\\tilde{d}^{(k)}_{i}-1,$  |\nL364: where $\\tau_{\\min}<0<\\tau_{\\max}$ are clipping bounds, set to $\\tau_{\\min}=-20$ and $\\tau_{\\max}=8$. This penalty is nonnegative and vanishes when the two policies assign the same probability to the demonstrated token. It has the form of the $k_{3}$ estimator of the KL divergence (cite187†Schulman, 2020 ), but it is evaluated on demonstrated tokens instead of samples from $\\pi^{(k)}$. It therefore anchors the probabilities of those tokens without constraining the full output distribution.\nL365: Own-corpus examples contribute only the SFT term, and anchor-corpus examples contribute only the anchoring term. We sample the anchor examples from $\\mathcal{D}_{\\setminus k}$ to match the size of $\\mathcal{D}_{k}$. The objective encourages each adapter to specialize to its own interface with less interference when the adapters are mixed.\nL366: ### A.3 Router Details\nL367: \nL368: The router is the two-layer perceptron of Eq. cite119†3 with hidden width $d_{h}=256$ and a SiLU nonlinearity. It has $d_{c}d_{h}+d_{h}+(d_{h}+1)K$ parameters, about $0.26$M for $d_{c}=1024$ and any $K\\leq 5$ used here. Its output layer starts at zero, so the initial mixture is uniform and the initial adapter is the mean of the bank. One coefficient vector is shared across all adapted matrices.\nL369: ### A.4 Training Hyperparameters\nL370: \nL371: Bank adapters use rank $r=16$ and scale $\\alpha=32$ on the attention and MLP projections, and the KL anchor uses $\\beta=1.0$ (Figure cite126†3 ). The router has hidden width $d_{h}=256$ and trains for 500 steps with AdamW. The scout’s encoder is BGE-large-en-v1.5 (cite188†Xiao et al., 2024 ) with $d_{c}=1024$. For domain transfer, the scout attempts $n=1$ task (§cite29†4.2 , RQ3). Table cite189†4 lists the hyperparameters of every component.\nL372: Component  | Setting  | Value\nL373: Bank adapters  | rank $r$ / scale $\\alpha$ / dropout  | 16 / 32 / 0\nL374: adapted matrices $\\Omega$  | attention q, k, v, o and MLP gate, up, down\nL375: epochs  | 6\nL376: learning rate and schedule  | $10^{-4}$, cosine, 10% warmup\nL377: effective batch size  | 4 (1 per device $\\times$ 4 accumulation)\nL378: precision and hardware  | bf16, DeepSpeed ZeRO-3, one 80 GB GPU\nL379: KL anchor  | weight $\\beta$  | 1.0\nL380: clipping $[\\tau_{\\min},\\tau_{\\max}]$  | $[-20,\\,8]$\nL381: anchor examples  | as many as own-corpus examples\nL382: Router  | hidden width $d_{h}$  | 256\nL383: optimizer  | AdamW, learning rate $10^{-4}$, no weight decay\nL384: schedule  | 20 linear warmup steps, then cosine\nL385: steps / batch size / gradient clip  | 500 / 16 / 1.0\nL386: maximum sequence length  | 8192 tokens\nL387: Scout  | explorer budget  | 25 pages, link depth 3\nL388: scouted tasks $n$ (domain transfer)  | 1\nL389: writer  | Qwen-3.5 9B, temperature 0\nL390: encoder  | BGE-large-en-v1.5, $d_{c}=1024$, 512 tokens\nL391: Agent decoding  | temperature  | 0.01\nL392: maximum new tokens per step  | 2048\nL393: Table 4: Training and evaluation hyperparameters.\nL394: ### A.5 Scout Details\nL395: ##### Explorer.\nL396: For version transfer, the explorer runs a scripted, task-free, breadth-first crawl of same-origin links from each site’s landing page. It visits up to $25$ pages per site at a maximum link depth of $3$. For every page it records the title, headings, link labels and targets, buttons, and form fields. It also performs one search probe, which submits the label of the first suitable link on the landing page to the first text or search field the crawl finds.\nL397: For domain transfer, the explorer is task-grounded instead. The frozen base policy, without an adapter, attempts $n$ tasks drawn from the training split of the site, and the explorer replays the pages that the policy visited.\nL398: ##### Writer.\nL399: A frozen language model writes the manual $m_{v}$ from the exploration record. The manual follows a fixed schema of seven sections, which cover purpose and top-level navigation, search, page anatomy, lists and pagination, forms and multi-step flows, interaction pitfalls, and visual style. The writer is instructed to use only labels and paths observed during exploration, and to omit comparisons with other interfaces and any assessment of task performance.\nL400: For version transfer, an interface spans several sites, and its manual concatenates the per-site manuals. For domain transfer, the writer also receives each attempted task’s intent and its reference procedure with the final answer removed. The manual then adds a deterministic eighth section that lists, for each attempted task, its intent and the sequence of actions with the visible label of each target.\nL401: ##### Encoder.\nL402: \nL403: A frozen sentence encoder $\\psi$ (BGE-large-en-v1.5 (cite188†Xiao et al., 2024 )) reads the tokens of the manual and returns their mean-pooled representation $\\psi(m_{v})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}$, with $d_{c}=1024$.\nL404: \nL405: ## Appendix B The T2L Baseline in Detail\nL406: \nL407: T2L (cite85†Charakorn et al., 2025 ) is a direct hypernetwork. It maps the same conditioning $h_{v}$ that WebMix uses to the two LoRA factors of every adapted matrix. We index the adapted matrices by $s\\in\\Omega$ in this appendix and the next.\nL408: ### B.1 Architecture\nL409: \nL410: A query addresses one factor of one adapted matrix through three learnable embedding tables, indexed by the matrix’s layer index $l_{s}$, its module type $m_{s}$, and the role $\\rho$ of the requested factor,\nL411: \nL412:  | $$z_{s,\\rho}\\;=\\;T_{\\phi}\\!\\Big(\\big[\\,h_{v};\\;E_{\\mathrm{lyr}}(l_{s});\\;E_{\\mathrm{mod}}(m_{s});\\;E_{\\mathrm{role}}(\\rho)\\,\\big]\\Big),\\qquad\\rho\\in\\{\\mathrm{A},\\mathrm{B}\\},$$  |  | (11)\nL413: where each embedding has width $32$ and the trunk $T_{\\phi}$ is a linear projection to width $d_{T}=128$ followed by a SiLU nonlinearity and two residual blocks. Output heads are shared at the coarsest granularity the target shapes allow. There is one linear head $P_{\\kappa}$ per distinct weight shape $(d^{\\mathrm{in}},d^{\\mathrm{out}})$ realized in the target set, shared across layers and across both roles.\nL414: The head emits $\\max(r\\,d^{\\mathrm{in}},\\,r\\,d^{\\mathrm{out}})$ values, and the leading block is reshaped into the requested factor,\nL415:  | $$A_{s}\\;=\\;\\mathrm{mat}_{r\\times d^{\\mathrm{in}}_{s}}\\!\\big(P_{\\kappa(s)}\\,z_{s,\\mathrm{A}}\\big),\\qquad B_{s}\\;=\\;\\mathrm{mat}_{d^{\\mathrm{out}}_{s}\\times r}\\!\\big(P_{\\kappa(s)}\\,z_{s,\\mathrm{B}}\\big).$$  |  | (12)\nL416: \nL417: Because layer, module, and role enter as embeddings, all $2|\\Omega|$ queries are batched and the full adapter is emitted in a single forward pass.\nL418: ### B.2 Reconstructor\nL419: T2L Recons follows the original training recipe of T2L (cite85†Charakorn et al., 2025 ). It regresses the stored bank adapters: for each source interface $v_{k}$, it minimizes the $\\ell_{1}$ distance between the generated update $B_{s}A_{s}$ and the stored update $\\Delta W^{(k)}_{s}$, scaled by $10^{4}$, over 16 randomly drawn matrices per step. It trains for 2000 steps with AdamW (learning rate $10^{-4}$, 100 warmup steps, cosine decay, gradient clipping at 1.0) from the initialization described below.\nL420: ### B.3 SFT\nL421: \nL422: T2L SFT trains the same generator end to end through the frozen policy with the router objective of Eq. cite190†8 , so it receives exactly the training signal of WebMix. It uses the router’s optimizer settings (Appendix cite40†A.4 ). Its output heads start at zero for $B$ and at a LoRA-style random bias for $A$, so training starts at the base policy.\nL423: ### B.4 Initialization at the oracle’s scale\nL424: A generator that writes weights must write them at the right order of magnitude from step $0$. Our trained per-version adapters have mean update magnitude $|\\Delta W|\\approx 5\\times 10^{-5}$. With every emitted entry i.i.d. $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_{0}^{2})$, an entry of $B_{s}A_{s}$ sums $r$ products of independent pairs, so its typical scale is $\\sqrt{r}\\,\\sigma_{0}^{2}$.\nL425: This is quadratic in $\\sigma_{0}$, which is why an innocuous-looking initialization can emit updates orders of magnitude too large and swamp the frozen computation. We therefore set $\\sigma_{0}=(\\varepsilon/\\sqrt{r})^{1/2}$ with $\\varepsilon=10^{-6}$, which places the generated update well below the oracle scale at initialization. It is near a no-op, but without the exactly-zero factor that would stall the other factor’s gradient through the product.\nL426: Where WebMix starts at the mean trained adapter, T2L starts from almost nothing.\nL427: ## Appendix C Analysis of the Bank and the Router\nL428: \nL429: This appendix answers RQ4 of §cite25†4 : what the bank adapters and the router’s mixtures look like.\nL430: ##### The bank.\nL431: Figure cite127†4(a) shows that the domain adapters of the WAL bank are close to orthogonal. Every pairwise cosine similarity of their updates is below 0.16, so each adapter contributes a distinct direction to the mixture. Figure cite128†5 measures the same similarity per layer. Adapters trained on the same domain become more similar with depth, reaching a cosine of about 0.5 (Qwen) and 0.6 (Llama) in the last layers.\nL432: Adapters of different domains stay below 0.14 with the KL anchor, and are about twice as similar without it. The anchor therefore keeps the bank’s adapters distinct, which is consistent with its gain in Figure cite126†3 .\nL433: ##### The router.\nL434: \nL435: Figure cite129†4(b) shows the weights the router assigns on TW versions. With v1 held out, the router builds its adapter mostly from v2 (81%) and v3 (17%), so the weights fall with distance from v1 in time. With v6 in the bank, it puts 96% of the weight on the v6 adapter. The coefficients can thus be read as the model’s judgment of which interfaces are similar. Appendix cite57†D.3 shows that, for a linearly independent bank, they are determined by the served adapter.\nL436: (a) Pairwise cosine of $\\Delta W$\nL437: \nL438: (b) Router mixture weights\nL439: Figure 4: The bank’s domain adapters are close to orthogonal, and the router mixes nearby versions (RQ4). (a) Pairwise cosine similarity of the weight updates $\\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA$ between the five domain adapters of the KL-anchored WebArena-Lite-v2 bank, computed over the whole model. All pairs stay below $0.16$. Reddit is the least aligned with the other domains in both models, while OSS overlaps most with Map (Qwen) and CMS (Llama).\nL440: (b) Mixture weights that the router assigns on TimeWarp versions. With v1 held out, it composes v1 mostly from the adjacent version v2 and then v3. With all six versions in the bank, it puts almost all weight on the matching v6 adapter.\n"
